APX.il = {"country":{"name":"Israel","tagline":"Bronze Age palace cellars, Rothschild's colonies and a modern industry that took winemaking up the Golan and into the desert.","classification":"Israel has an official list of wine regions (Galil with the Golan, Upper and Lower Galilee subregions; Shomron; Shimshon; the Judean Hills; and the Negev) used on labels, but no appellation system with yield or grape rules. Kosher certification (and mevushal status) is the most visible label distinction."},"regions":[{"name":"Galilee and Golan Heights","slug":"galilee-and-golan-heights","tagline":"The coolest, highest vineyards: basalt on the Golan, limestone in the Upper Galilee.","ids":["il-galilee","il-golan-heights"]},{"name":"Judean Hills","slug":"judean-hills","tagline":"Terraced limestone around Jerusalem and Domaine du Castel.","ids":["il-judean-hills"]},{"name":"Shomron","slug":"shomron","tagline":"Carmel's historic heartland around Zichron Ya'akov and Binyamina.","ids":["il-shomron"]},{"name":"Shimshon","slug":"shimshon","tagline":"Between the coast and the Judean Hills, where Rishon LeZion's winery began.","ids":["il-shimshon"]},{"name":"Negev","slug":"negev","tagline":"Desert vineyards on ancient Nabataean terraces.","ids":["il-negev"]}],"items":{"il-galilee":{"name":"Galilee (Galil)","cat":"Wine region","region":"Galilee and Golan Heights","created":"Israel's regional map of five wine regions","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Carignan","summary":"The Galilee is Israel's northern wine region and its quality heartland. The Upper Galilee, around Dalton, Kerem Ben Zimra and the slopes near the Lebanese border, rises to about 800 metres on limestone, terra rossa and basalt, while the Lower Galilee spreads around Mount Tabor at lower altitude.\n\nCool nights at altitude, winter rain and long dry summers suit Bordeaux and Rhône varieties, and the region supplies many of Israel's best-known wineries, including those based elsewhere that buy Galilee fruit.","terroir":"Limestone, terra rossa and volcanic soils; 300 to 800 m; Mediterranean climate with winter rain and dry summers.","producers":["Dalton","Galil Mountain","Adir","Tabor","Recanati"],"nerd_note":"Galilee fruit is the backbone of many Israeli blends labelled simply 'Galilee', the largest appellation in the country by quality output.","rslug":"galilee-and-golan-heights"},"il-golan-heights":{"name":"Golan Heights","cat":"Wine region","region":"Galilee and Golan Heights","created":"Subregion of Israel's Galil region","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Gewürztraminer, Pinot Noir","summary":"The Golan Heights plateau rises from about 400 metres near the Sea of Galilee to around 1,200 metres below Mount Hermon, on volcanic basalt and tuff. It is the coolest and highest vineyard land under Israeli control, with snow some winters and big day-night temperature swings.\n\nThe Golan was captured by Israel from Syria in the 1967 war and annexed in 1981; most of the world, including the UN Security Council, does not recognise the annexation, although the United States did in 2019. Under a 2015 European Commission notice, upheld by the EU Court of Justice in the Psagot case (2019), wine from Israeli settlements in territories occupied since 1967 may not be labelled 'Product of Israel' in the EU and must state its territory and that it comes from a settlement.","terroir":"Basalt and volcanic tuff; 400 to 1,200 m; cool nights, winter snow on the heights.","producers":["Golan Heights Winery (Yarden)","Pelter","Bazelet HaGolan","Assaf"],"nerd_note":"The Golan Heights Winery released its first wine, a 1983 Yarden Sauvignon Blanc, and is often credited with starting Israel's quality revolution.","rslug":"galilee-and-golan-heights"},"il-judean-hills":{"name":"Judean Hills","cat":"Wine region","region":"Judean Hills","created":"One of Israel's five wine regions","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Syrah, Chardonnay","summary":"The Judean Hills around Jerusalem rise to 600 to 1,000 metres on limestone, chalk and terra rossa. Vineyards on terraces, some reusing ancient walls, enjoy cool nights and sea breezes from the west, and the region has become home to many of Israel's leading boutique wineries.\n\nPart of the region as mapped by Israel lies in the occupied West Bank, around Gush Etzion. Under a 2015 European Commission notice, upheld by the EU Court of Justice in the Psagot case (2019), wine from Israeli settlements in territories occupied since 1967 may not be labelled 'Product of Israel' in the EU and must state its territory and that it comes from a settlement.","terroir":"Limestone, chalk and terra rossa; terraces at 600 to 1,000 m; cool nights.","producers":["Castel","Flam","Tzora","Clos de Gat","Sphera"],"nerd_note":"The Book of Numbers tells of spies who carried a single cluster of grapes on a pole from the valley of Eshkol, near Hebron, an image Israel's tourism ministry adopted as its logo.","rslug":"judean-hills"},"il-shomron":{"name":"Shomron (Samaria)","cat":"Wine region","region":"Shomron","created":"One of Israel's five wine regions","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Carignan, Petite Sirah, Chardonnay","summary":"Shomron runs from the Mount Carmel ridge and the Sharon plain around Zichron Ya'akov and Binyamina inland into the hills of Samaria. The coastal section has warm summers tempered by sea breezes; the hills are higher and cooler.\n\nThe inland part of the region lies in the occupied West Bank. Under a 2015 European Commission notice, upheld by the EU Court of Justice in the Psagot case (2019), wine from Israeli settlements in territories occupied since 1967 may not be labelled 'Product of Israel' in the EU and must state its territory and that it comes from a settlement.","terroir":"Limestone and terra rossa on the Carmel; alluvial soils near the coast; inland hills.","producers":["Carmel (Zichron Ya'akov)","Tishbi","Margalit","Binyamina"],"nerd_note":"Zichron Ya'akov's cellars, built under Baron Edmond de Rothschild's patronage, opened in 1892 and still operate as part of Carmel.","rslug":"shomron"},"il-shimshon":{"name":"Shimshon (Samson)","cat":"Wine region","region":"Shimshon","created":"One of Israel's five wine regions","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Carignan, Syrah, Chardonnay","summary":"Shimshon, named after the biblical Samson, covers the central coastal plain and the Judean foothills (Shfela) between the sea and the Judean Hills, including the Ayalon valley. It is warmer and lower than the hills and grows large volumes for Israel's big wineries.","terroir":"Coastal plain and foothills; alluvial and limestone soils; warm, humid summers.","producers":["Barkan","Latrun Monastery","Carmel"],"nerd_note":"Trappist monks at Latrun, in the Ayalon valley, have made wine since the late nineteenth century.","rslug":"shimshon"},"il-negev":{"name":"Negev","cat":"Wine region","region":"Negev","created":"One of Israel's five wine regions","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Syrah, Chardonnay","summary":"The Negev desert covers the south of Israel. With under 100 to 300 millimetres of rain a year, vineyards depend on drip irrigation, sometimes with brackish water, and on cold desert nights at altitude around Mitzpe Ramon and Ramat Negev.","terroir":"Loess and sand; desert climate with hot days and cold nights; drip irrigation.","producers":["Carmey Avdat","Nana Estate"],"nerd_note":"Byzantine-era wine presses at Shivta and Avdat show the desert towns produced wine traded across the Mediterranean as 'Gaza wine'.","rslug":"negev"}}};
